+# Backing Up Your App
+
+This guide covers backups for your self-hosted instance. Read it before you need it.
+
+## Why Volume Backups Beat In-App Exports
+
+In-app exports capture only what the app knows about. They skip config files,
+uploaded media, and environment settings. A volume-level backup copies the whole
+filesystem the app writes to. You get the database, uploads, and settings in one
+snapshot. Restores become a file copy, not an import ritual.
+
+## Weekly Schedule
+
+Run a full volume backup every week. Follow these rules:
+
+- Pick a quiet hour. Sunday at 02:00 works for most apps.
+- Stop the app, or use filesystem snapshots, so the copy is consistent.
+- Keep four weekly snapshots. Add a monthly snapshot for six months of history.
+- Store one copy off-site. A second disk in the same rack is not a backup.
+
+Automate the job with cron or your container host's scheduler. Check the log
+every Monday. A silent failure means you have no backups.
+
+## Restore Drill
+
+A backup you have never restored is a hope, not a plan. Run a drill every quarter.
+
+1. Spin up a fresh host or VM.
+2. Install the same app version you run in production.
+3. Copy your latest snapshot onto the new data volume.
+4. Start the app and log in.
+5. Check recent records, uploaded files, and user accounts.
+6. Time the drill. Record how long full recovery takes.
+
+Fix anything that breaks. Update this guide when the steps change.
